data.content:
For your file system statics (`File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, `Directory.Exists`), the best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and provides a drop-in `IFileSystem` interface.

## Setup

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

## Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

## Inject into your classes

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, "content");

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

## Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports", new MockDirectoryData() }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
Assert.Contains("sales", mockFs.File.ReadAllText(path));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, and `DriveInfo` in one interface with a ready-made `MockFileSystem` for tests — no need to hand-roll anything.

Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow` and `Environment.*`. For those on .NET 10:
- **Time** → inject `TimeProvider` (built-in): `builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);`
- **Environment** → create a small custom `IEnvironmentProvider` wrapping only `GetEnvironmentVariable` and `MachineName`.

